  "hypothesis": "weakening",
  "design": "The framework started in January 2012 everywhere except WA (August 2012): 2011-12 childcare entry and exit in the seven early jurisdictions against WA, with synthetic DiD weights.",
  "comparison": "NSW/VIC/QLD/SA/TAS/ACT/NT from 2012 against WA",
  "window": "2011-12 to 2011-12",
  "verdict": "inconclusive",
  "finding_label": "No detectable effect",
  "grade": "Moderate",
  "grade_reason": "design checks passed; registered layer and checks agree",
  "headline": "No detectable effect: business entry rate −0.3 per 100 businesses a year (p = 0.85), synthetic DiD, grade Moderate.",
  "category_summaries": {
    "competition": "Business entry rate: −0.3 per 100 businesses a year (p = 0.85; detectable from 6.0). Business exit rate: −1.9 per 100 businesses a year (p = 0.049; detectable from 2.2).",
    "prices": "Child care: prices relative to the CPI: +4.4% (log points) (p = 0.075; detectable from 5.0).",
    "productivity": "Not measured in this evaluation."
  },
  "key_insights": [
    "The question: did the National Quality Framework for childcare change how many childcare businesses opened or closed in its first year (2011-12)?",
    "The comparison: The framework started in January 2012 everywhere except WA (August 2012): 2011-12 childcare entry and exit in the seven early jurisdictions against WA, with synthetic DiD weights.",
    "What we found: no detectable change. Business entry rate: −0.3 per 100 businesses a year (90% interval −2.3 to +2.4; p = 0.85).",
    "Other outcomes: business exit rate −1.9 (p = 0.049); child care: prices relative to the CPI +4.4 (p = 0.075). Secondary outcomes are outside the registered decision rule and not adjusted for testing several outcomes, so a p-value below 0.05 among them is a lead, not a finding.",
    "How precise: only changes larger than about 6.0 per 100 businesses a year could have been detected.",
    "Robustness (not pre-registered): across 9 variants (each state left out, other windows and pre-periods, weights and data vintages) the business entry rate estimate keeps its sign in 8, reaches p < 0.05 in 0, and ranges from −0.8 to +0.3.",
    "Compared with the earlier evaluation (2026-10-15, now archived): it found no detectable effect (business entry rate −0.3 per 100 businesses a year). The conclusion is unchanged; the smallest detectable effect is now 6.0, down from 9.8. This evaluation uses the estimator chosen in the blinded power study and the latest data.",
    "What it means: no effect is detectable with public state-level data; an effect smaller than the detectable size cannot be ruled out."
  ],
  "method": {
    "summary": "Synthetic DiD on ANZSIC classes by state and financial year, the layer chosen for this reform in the blinded power study, with every other layer reported side by side.",
    "equations": [
      {
        "label": "Synthetic DiD",
        "text": "hat{τ} = arg min over (μ, α, β, τ) of Σ_{i,t} (y_{it} − μ − α_{i} − β_{t} − D_{it}·τ)^{2} · hat{ω}_{i} · hat{λ}_{t}"
      },
      {
        "label": "Year by year",
        "text": "y_{cst} = α_{cs} + δ_{ct} + γ_{st} + Σ_{k≠−1} β_{k}·1[t − T_{s} = k]·T_{cs} + ε_{cst}"
      }
    ],
    "terms": [
      {
        "symbol": "y_{cst}",
        "meaning": "Entries (or exits) per 100 businesses operating at the start of year t, class c, state s"
      },
      {
        "symbol": "D_{cst}",
        "meaning": "1 for the reform's class in a treated state (or city) from its first exposed period to the end of its window"
      },
      {
        "symbol": "hat{ω}_{i}",
        "meaning": "weights on comparison cells, chosen so their pre-reform path matches the treated group's"
      },
      {
        "symbol": "hat{λ}_{t}",
        "meaning": "weights on pre-reform periods, chosen to resemble the post-reform periods"
      }
    ],
    "estimand": "The average change in the outcome for the reform's classes in treated states over the window, beyond the same classes elsewhere and the treated states' other classes, net of the mean placebo estimate.",
    "identification": "Without the reform, the treated classes in treated states would have moved like the same classes elsewhere plus their states' other classes (parallel trends in the saturated model; for synthetic DiD, like the weighted comparison cells).",
    "inference": "The same estimator applied to up to 40 placebo industry groups (or CPI classes) at the reform's states and dates gives the no-effect distribution: the p-value is the share of centred placebo estimates at least as large; the 90% interval subtracts their 5th and 95th percentiles. Benjamini-Hochberg q-values across the specification's primary outcomes. Placebo states and fake dates check the reform's own classes.",
    "steps": [
      "Registered the layer, outcomes, windows and placebo pools in the specification before any treated post-reform outcome was computed.",
      "Design stage: hid treated cells from first exposure, computed every layer's placebo distribution, the own-class placebos and the pre-reform event-study check.",
      "Committed the design and plan, ran a blinded dry run, then one registered run with treated cells restored.",
      "Judged the registered layer's estimate against its placebo distribution; reported every other layer side by side."
    ]
  },
